Let me look at the EndpointArgs type more carefully:

```ts
export type EndpointArgs<D> = D extends {
  pathParams: infer P extends readonly string[];
}
  ? D extends { bodyBuilder: (...args: infer BA) => unknown }
    ? [...PathParamArgs<P>, ...BA]
    : D extends { hasBody: true }
      ? [...PathParamArgs<P>, body: object]
      : D extends { queryParams: Record<string, string> }
        ? [...PathParamArgs<P>, ...(string | number | undefined)[]]
        : PathParamArgs<P>
  : D extends { bodyBuilder: (...args: infer BA) => unknown }
    ? BA
    : D extends { hasBody: true }
      ? [body: object]
      : D extends { queryParams: Record<string, string> }
        ? (string | number | undefined)[]
```

The type definition is handling different combinations of endpoint configurations—checking for path parameters first, then falling back to body builders, explicit body objects, query parameters, or just returning the path parameter args when none of those apply.

The problem is that `bodyBuilder` takes precedence over `queryParams`, so when both are present together, the type only captures the body builder arguments and ignores the query parameters entirely. The runtime code actually interleaves query parameters between path parameters and body builder arguments, but the type system doesn't reflect this ordering. I need to update the EndpointArgs type to properly handle the case where both `queryParams` and `bodyBuilder` exist, ensuring the final argument tuple includes path parameters, query parameters, and body builder arguments in the correct sequence.

